# Chaos: HD sprite overlay, a finer picture for a sprite id in the same rectangle (O539; client/BASE.md "0196"; src/client/chaoshdoverlay.h (new, pure),
# spritemanager.h/.cpp, thingtype.cpp, luafunctions_client.cpp, mods/client_settings dataset.lua and options/graphics.otui; after 0001-0195).
# The 32 px sprites of the pair and the .dat / .spr stay as they are. A parallel overlay (data/hd/index.txt and atlas PNGs, made by
# client/tools/sprites/gen_hd_overlay.py) maps a sprite id to a square tile of an atlas, 32 * scale px a side (scale 2 or 4).
#  * Hook: SpriteManager::getSpriteImage, the place 0027 (xBRZ) builds a sprite's image. On a cache miss a sprite with a tile gets that
#    tile instead of the 32 px sprite or its xBRZ copy, resampled to the size one sprite has now (spriteSize: 32, or 64 with HD Sprite
#    Upscaling): a 64 px tile on 64 is a copy, a 128 px tile (4x) on 64 is the mean of 2 x 2 (alpha weighted), a 64 px tile on 32 the
#    mean as well, a smaller tile on a bigger size is nearest. So at every size the engine draws a sprite the rectangle is the same as
#    before and the thing builders see the size they always see; the detail shows with HD Sprite Upscaling on
#    (the PC default).
#  * Slots: a sprite id cannot reach a tile the pair leaves empty (id 0), a sprite that other things draw too, or a sprite one thing repeats
#    with another picture. The index has `slot <category> <thing id> <sprite index> <sprite id> <atlas> <x> <y> <scale>` records for those
#    places; ThingType::getTexture (its one in-game sprite loop) asks g_sprites.getHdSlotImage(category, id, index, sprite id) before the
#    sprite, and a slot whose sprite id is not the one the pair has there now is ignored. A slot wins over a `sprite` record.
#  * Safety: the index stores the crc32 of the 32x32 RGBA bytes of the pair's sprite the tile was cut for; a sprite id that a later
#    pair gave another picture shows the plain sprite (one log line; 00000000 = unchecked). A missing or bad index or atlas draws
#    the plain sprites, nothing else changes.
#  * Lua: g_sprites.loadHdOverlay(dir) (dir/index.txt, atlases next to it, read when a sprite is first drawn) returns the number of
#    mapped sprites; unloadHdOverlay(), setHdOverlayEnabled(bool), isHdOverlayEnabled(), getHdOverlayCount(), hasHdSprite(id).
#    Creatures only for now (the art), but the key is a sprite id, so items and effects work the same.
#  * Option "HD monsters" (dataset.lua `hdMonsters`, default on, Options > Graphics under HD Sprite Upscaling): its apply tells the
#    overlay module game_hdsprites (onOption), which sets the switch before the sprites load; changing it asks for a restart like
#    hdmodeBox. The module turns it off once on a phone (g_app.isMobile), like the phone defaults of game_visualfx.
# Cost: one tile cut and resampled per sprite first drawn, then the image cache of 0027 holds it; no per frame work.
# Checked: applies after 0001-0195 on the base client tree (no fuzz); spritemanager.cpp, thingtype.cpp and luafunctions_client.cpp pass
# g++ -std=c++20 -fsyntax-only; the header passes client/tools/chaoshdoverlay_test.cpp. Not built, linked or run in a client.
